[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Low-level requirements of the web application item, written from the code as built: every module in
`frontend/lib/`, `frontend/i18n/request.ts`, and the components that the unit tests in `frontend/test/` exercise
(`components/shared/DeltaBadge.tsx`, `components/shared/UnitsToggle.tsx`,
`components/three/flightPathGeometry.ts`). Pages and the other components are verified at HLR level in this issue
(decision D-10, open item OI-012). Parents are in `hlr-web.sdoc`. The design is
`docs/lifecycle/06-design/lld-web.md`; the interface is `docs/lifecycle/03-system/icd/icd-web-ui.md`. Where the
code does less than its HLR asks, or differs from it, the COMMENT says so and `lld-web.md` section 6 lists it.
<<<

[[SECTION]]
TITLE: API client (frontend/lib/api.ts)

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-001
STATUS: Active
TITLE: API address in the browser
STATEMENT: >>>
`API_URL` is `NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL` when that build-time variable is set and not empty; otherwise the empty string
(the page's own origin) when the code runs in a browser on an `https:` page, and `http://localhost:8000` in every
other case (an `http:` page, or the server render). One trailing `/` is removed. Every browser call of this module
goes to `<API_URL><path>`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test sets the environment or the page protocol; the tests only use the resolved value to address their mock
server. A review reads the `API_URL` expression.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-027

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-002
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Request time limit and connection failure
STATEMENT: >>>
`request(path, init, timeout)` calls `fetch` with its own abort signal and aborts it after `timeout` ms (default
20,000 ms; 60,000 ms for downloads and AI questions). An abort of the caller's own signal aborts the request too.
When `fetch` rejects: if the caller's signal was aborted the original error is re-thrown; if the time limit fired
it throws `ApiError` with status 0 and code `timeout`; otherwise `ApiError` with status 0 and code `network`. The
timer covers the wait for the response headers only; it is cleared once `fetch` resolves.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
The polling test drives a refused connection through this function but checks only the poller's end state, not
the error code; no test runs into the time limit. A review reads `request`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-024

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-003
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Error object and the server's detail text
STATEMENT: >>>
`ApiError(status, message, code)` has the message `message` when that is not empty, else `code`, else
`HTTP <status>`, and keeps `code` only when there is no server message. For a response that is not ok the client
throws `ApiError(status, detail, fallback)`, where `fallback` is the call's key under `errors` in
`messages/en.json` and `detail` is: the body's `detail` when it is a string; the `msg` of the first element when
`detail` is a list; otherwise (including a body that is not JSON) null. An ok response returns its parsed JSON
body.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test, Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
The polling test checks that a 422 with a string `detail` reaches the caller as the error text. The list form and
the non-JSON body are checked by reading `detail` and `ApiError`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-024

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-004
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Airframe-scoped requests carry and keep the token
STATEMENT: >>>
`airframeRequest` sends the token stored for the label (LLR-WEB-029), when there is one, in the request header
`X-Airframe-Token`, and never in the address; the label is URL-encoded in the path. An answer 401 throws
`AirframeAuthError` with code `airframeNeedsToken`, an answer 403 with code `airframeWrongToken`, each with the
server's detail text when given; `unclaimed` is true when that text matches `not claimed` (case ignored). Any other
failure throws `ApiError` with the call's fallback code. On success a non-empty string `airframe_token` in the body
is stored for the label.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test exercises airframe calls. A review reads `airframeHeaders`, `keepToken` and `airframeRequest`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-017

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-005
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Airframe calls
STATEMENT: >>>
Through `airframeRequest` (20 s limit), with `<label>` URL-encoded: `getTrends` is `GET /trends/<label>` (fallback
code `trends`); `getAirframeConfig` is `GET /airframe/<label>/config` (`airframeConfig`); `updateAirframeConfig` is
`PUT /airframe/<label>/config` with a JSON body of any of `checklist_items`, `maintenance_interval_hours`,
`alert_rules`, `webhook_url` (`airframeUpdate`); `addMaintenanceEntry` is `POST /airframe/<label>/maintenance`
with `date`, `maintenance_type` and optional `notes` (`maintenance`); `recordAppliedChanges` is `POST
/airframe/<label>/changes` with the change record as given (`changesSave`); `getAppliedChanges` is `GET
/airframe/<label>/changes` (`changes`). The bodies are sent as `Content-Type: application/json`.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test calls these functions. A review reads them against the API's interface.
<<<
