[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-006
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Upload request
STATEMENT: >>>
`uploadLog(file, onProgress, options)` sends one `XMLHttpRequest` `POST <API_URL>/analyse` with a multipart body:
`file`; `airframe_label` with the trimmed label when that is not empty; `contribute_to_dataset` = `true` only when
`contribute` is set; `planning_brief_sha256` lower-cased when a brief hash is given (this function does not check
its form). When the label is not empty and a token is stored for it, the token goes in the header
`X-Airframe-Token`. The time limit is 600,000 ms. On each upload progress event with a known length it calls
`onProgress` with the percentage sent, rounded to a whole number. An abort of `options.signal` aborts the request.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No unit test runs an upload; the end-to-end smoke test sends one file through it but checks only the page that
follows. A review reads `uploadLog`.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-007
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Upload answers and failures
STATEMENT: >>>
`uploadLog` resolves with the parsed body on status 200 or 202 and then stores an `airframe_token` in it for the
label; a body that is not JSON rejects with code `invalidResponse`. It rejects: on 401 or 403 with
`AirframeAuthError` code `airframeBelongs`; on 413 with code `tooLarge`; on 429 with `uploadLimit`; on 503 with
`busy` and no server text, whatever the server sent; on any other status with `server`. Except for 503 the
server's string `detail`, when present, is the message. A connection error rejects with `network`, the time limit
with `uploadTimeout`, an abort with `uploadCancelled`, each with status 0.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
VERIFICATION_NOTE: >>>
No test exercises the failure branches. A review reads the `load`, `error`, `timeout` and `abort` handlers.
<<<
ALLOCATION: web
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: HLR-WEB-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: LLR-WEB-008
STATUS: Active
TITLE: Status and report calls
STATEMENT: >>>
`getStatus(id)` is `GET /status/<id>` and returns the body (fallback code `status`). `getReport(id)` is `GET
/report/<id>`; it returns null on 202 (report not yet written), the report on any other ok status, and throws
`ApiError` with fallback code `report` otherwise. Both URL-encode the id, use the 20 s limit and pass the caller's
abort signal.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Test
